
# System Design Document: Machine Learning Churn Prediction Engine

A high-level architecture and system design specification for predicting bank customer churn using baseline **Logistic Regression** and an **Artificial Neural Network (ANN)**.

---

## 1. System Architecture Overview

The pipeline processes raw tabular customer records, applies feature transformation and normalization pipelines, trains candidate classification models, and generates inference outputs.

```
+------------------+     +-----------------------+     +------------------------+
| Data Source      | --> | Preprocessing &       | --> | Feature Engineering &  |
| Churn_Modelling  |     | Cleaning              |     | Scaling                |
+------------------+     +-----------------------+     +------------------------+
                                                                   |
                                                                   v
+------------------+     +-----------------------+     +------------------------+
| Inference &      | <-- | Model Evaluation &    | <-- | Model Training         |
| Decision Metrics |     | Metrics Benchmarking  |     | (Logistic & Neural Net)|
+------------------+     +-----------------------+     +------------------------+
```

---

## 2. Component Specifications

### 2.1 Ingestion & Cleaning Layer
* **Source Dataset**: `Churn_Modelling.csv` ($N = 10,000$ records, $14$ attributes).
* **Data Integrity Checks**: Row validation ensuring non-null entries (`data.isnull().sum() == 0`).
* **Column Pruning**: Drops non-predictive keys (`RowNumber`, `CustomerId`, `Surname`).

### 2.2 Preprocessing & Feature Engineering
* **Categorical Encoding**:
  * **Gender**: Binary Label Encoding ($\text{Male} \rightarrow 1, \text{Female} \rightarrow 0$).
  * **Geography**: Multi-class One-Hot Encoding via `ColumnTransformer(drop='first')` to resolve the dummy variable trap.
* **Feature Scaling**: Z-score normalization applied via `StandardScaler`:
  $$z = \frac{x - \mu}{\sigma}$$

